We have a number of critical bugs that the pending release
fixes.  These include errors in correlation matrix Jacobian,
buggy Cholesky factor parser, several RNG bugs, and memory
leaks in the categorical distributions and softmax functions.
We also have a gazillion more features in this release.

Is there a design everyone agrees on somewhere for which new functions
you want?  Is it just changes to existing functions (for what I've been
calling "make-like" and you've been calling "Sebastian's function(s?)").

And is there someone in line to code, test, and document it?  I
don't understand enough R to get anywhere near this.  I'm butting
out of the design and doc, as well.

If so, what's the time estimate on getting it implemented (not hours
it'll take, but time from now in calendar days before it'll be done)?

If the answer to that last question isn't something like a week
or less, then I think we should wait until after 2.3 in order to
get the bug fixes out.

I hope to then get L-BFGS done soon and put out another release
soon after 2.3.  (Speaking of which, is the finite diffs Hessian
ready to go with that?)

Then after that, I have the ODE solver to do.  But that probably won't
be ready for at least five or six weeks.

This is just my opinion, of course --- what do others think?
I know Marcus wants to get L-BFGS in ASAP and I think Andrew wants
it, even if he doesn't know it.

- Bob

