Yes, I think that (and the verbose option) is all.  At some point we’re going to want to do more for stan_mle() but I’m still doing research on that.

Also at some point we should discuss (with Bob the linguist) our naming conventions.  If we have stan_optimize() and stan_mle() but lm_stan(), that’s a bit awkward…

On Jun 18, 2014, at 5:42 PM, Ben Goodrich <goodri...@gmail.com> wrote:

> On Wednesday, June 18, 2014 11:29:18 AM UTC-4, Andrew Gelman wrote:
> > Right now, I have to use Sebastian’s stan_run().  If I use the current stan(), I need to first run it with chains=0 and ignore the warning messages, give that object a name, then use that name in later stan calls. I just want to do stan(“foo.stan”) and have it compile only as needed.  Similarly for stan_optimize().
>
> We can have a save option for stan() and stan_optimize() and look for a compiled object on disk. What I meant to ask was, are you asking for anything (else) different with the default behavior of stan(), which compiles the model and runs four chains?
>
> > Or are the names stan() and stan_optimize() a violation of parallelism?  I’m assuming that’s ok because doing full Bayes is our default option.
>
> Should be fine. We just have to get rid of optimizing(), which may break some users' code.
>
> Ben
>
> On Jun 18, 2014, at 5:23 PM, Ben Goodrich <goodri...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > > How is that different from what stan() does now?
> > >
> > > On Wednesday, June 18, 2014 11:20:14 AM UTC-4, Andrew Gelman wrote:
> > > > What’s important to me is that we can use stan() and stan_optimize() without having to add code in our scripts to do pre-compiling.
> > > >
> > > > On Jun 18, 2014, at 5:01 PM, Ben Goodrich <goodri...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > >
> > > > > I'll try to implement the save argument tonight. Having additional functions that only differ in their default behavior from existing functions that users should already be aware of is confusing I think.
> > > > >
> > > > > Ben
> > > > >
> > > > > On Wednesday, June 18, 2014 10:39:26 AM UTC-4, Andrew Gelman wrote:
> > > > > > Hi, Ben.  I was thinking that stan_save() would have certain default behavior, so that the model foo.stan would be saved as foo.rda or whatever.  I wanted to make things as smooth as possible.  If we had the “save” argument in the stan() and stan_optimize() functions then that might work too, as then these could call the regular save() functions.  As I was saying, for me it is awkward to have big objects hanging around in the workspace. Indeed I’ve sometimes been told that it is good R practice to _not_ rely on things being in the workspace but instead to have scripts be self-contained.  As a user, I really would like to just use stan() and stan_optimize() without having to use a different stan_compile() function the first time a model is called.
