But that’s exactly how you—don’t— want to do it!  A pull request is a discussion, and if there are questions the submitter can answer them.  Having a test in place won’t answer anything down the road either.

> I just see tests as a way of informing someone wanting to change it some unspecified amount of time in the future. The actual pull request, although not lost in theory, will be lost in practice. The tests would exist.

But what would they say?  That something changed — we know that already from the diff.  And the test
doesn’t make it clear why it should not be changed.  Tests can validate spec but that are a bad replacement
for doc.

